---
/**
 * Gratuity under the Code on Social Security: a small calculator plus the rules, with every table
 * computed at build time by the engine.
 */
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import NumberField from '../components/form/NumberField.astro';
import Segmented from '../components/form/Segmented.astro';
import { bracketPath } from '../lib/brackets';
import { gratuityData } from '../lib/explainers';
import { formatRupees } from '../lib/format';
import { renderGratuity } from '../lib/gratuity-result';

const data = gratuityData();
const initial = renderGratuity({
  wages: data.serviceWages,
  years: 5,
  months: 0,
  employment: 'permanent',
});
const lakh = (ctc: number) => `₹${(ctc / 1_00_000).toFixed(2).replace(/\.?0+$/, '')} lakh`;
const perYear = (data.serviceWages * 15) / 26;
const maximum = formatRupees(data.maximum);
---

<DocLayout
  title="Gratuity calculator and the rules behind it"
  description="Work out your gratuity under the Code on Social Security: 15/26 of a month's wages for each year, payable after five years, or one year for fixed-term staff, up to ₹20 lakh."
  updated="2026-10-09"
  ogImage="gratuity-calculator"
  about={['Gratuity', 'Code on Social Security 2020', 'Fixed-term employment']}
>
  <p>
    Gratuity is a lump sum your employer pays when you leave after enough years of service. Since 21
    November 2025 it is governed by the Code on Social Security, which brought in two changes: it is
    worked out on Labour Code wages rather than Basic alone, and fixed-term employees now qualify
    after one year instead of five.
  </p>

  <form id="gratuity" class="panel overflow-clip" novalidate aria-label="Gratuity calculator">
    <div class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-5 p-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <NumberField
        id="g-wages"
        label="Wages a month"
        value={data.serviceWages.toLocaleString('en-IN')}
        prefix="₹"
        width="7rem"
        hint="Basic and DA, plus anything the 50% rule adds back"
        money
      />
      <Segmented
        name="g-type"
        legend="Employment"
        options={[
          { value: 'permanent', label: 'Permanent' },
          { value: 'fixed-term', label: 'Fixed-term' },
        ]}
        value="permanent"
      />
      <NumberField id="g-years" label="Years of service" value="5" width="3.5rem" />
      <NumberField id="g-months" label="And months" value="0" width="3.5rem" />
    </div>
    <output
      id="g-result"
      for="g-wages g-years g-months"
      class="block border-t border-line bg-[linear-gradient(180deg,var(--color-accent-soft),var(--color-surface))] p-5 sm:p-6"
      aria-live="polite"
      set:html={initial}
    />
  </form>

  <h2>The formula</h2>
  <p>
    For each year of service you get <strong>15 days' wages</strong>, worked out on your last
    month's wages. For someone paid monthly, 15 days' wages is the monthly figure times 15/26,
    because a month is treated as 26 working days.
  </p>
  <p>
    At {formatRupees(data.serviceWages)} of wages a month, as at {data.serviceLpa} LPA with Basic at
    half of CTC, one year is worth {formatRupees(perYear)}. Five years is{' '}
    {formatRupees(perYear * 5)}.
  </p>
  <p>
    The wages are Labour Code wages: Basic and DA, plus whatever the{' '}
    <a href="/labour-code-wages">50% rule</a> adds back when allowances are more than half of your
    pay. A calculator that uses Basic alone will understate gratuity whenever Basic is below half of
    CTC.
  </p>

  <h2>When you qualify</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Permanent employees</strong> need five years of continuous service.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fixed-term employees</strong> engaged directly by the employer qualify after one year
      and are paid pro rata for the time they actually served.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Contract workers</strong> engaged through a contractor still need five years.
    </li>
    <li>
      On <strong>death or disablement</strong> there is no minimum period.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Service</th>
          <th scope="col">Permanent</th>
          <th scope="col">Fixed-term</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.service.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{s.label}</td>
            <td>{s.permanent > 0 ? formatRupees(s.permanent) : 'Nothing yet'}</td>
            <td>{s.fixedTerm > 0 ? formatRupees(s.fixedTerm) : 'Nothing yet'}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    On wages of {formatRupees(data.serviceWages)} a month. For a permanent employee a part year
    counts as a full year only when it is more than six months: 5 years 6 months pays{' '}
    {formatRupees(data.rounding.atSixMonths)}, but 5 years 7 months pays{' '}
    {formatRupees(data.rounding.atSevenMonths)}. Some courts treated 4 years and 240 days as five
    years under the old Payment of Gratuity Act; whether that carries over under the Code is not
    settled, so the calculator counts full years.
  </p>

  <h2>What it adds up to</h2>
  <p>
    Gratuity on leaving after each length of service, using the calculator's standard structure with
    Basic at half of CTC, and assuming your salary stays the same throughout. In practice it is
    worked out on your last wages, so pay rises raise it for every year you have served.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CTC</th>
          {data.years.map((y) => (
            <th scope="col">{y} years</th>
          ))}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={bracketPath(r.lpa)}>{r.lpa} LPA</a>
            </td>
            {r.byYears.map((y) => (
              <td>
                {formatRupees(y.amount)}
                {y.capped && <span class="block text-xs text-ink-3">The maximum</span>}
              </td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The Code caps gratuity at {maximum}. With this structure, 20 years of service reaches the cap
    above a CTC of about {lakh(data.capAbove)}.
  </p>

  <h2>Gratuity inside your CTC</h2>
  <p>
    Most offer letters include a gratuity line in CTC: 15/26 of a month's wages for every year,
    about 4.8% of wages. It is not paid to you each month. It is what your employer sets aside, and
    you only receive gratuity when you leave after qualifying, so the calculator leaves it out of
    in-hand pay.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CTC</th>
          <th scope="col">Wages a month</th>
          <th scope="col">Gratuity in CTC a year</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={bracketPath(r.lpa)}>{r.lpa} LPA</a>
            </td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.wages)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.provision)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Tax on gratuity</h2>
  <p>
    For most private-sector employees, gratuity is tax-free up to {maximum} across your whole
    working life; anything above that is taxed as salary in the year you receive it. Gratuity paid
    to Central and State Government employees is fully exempt. The exact exempt amount depends on a
    formula in the Income-tax Act, 2025, so if your gratuity is large, check the figure on your Form
    130 (formerly Form 16).
  </p>

  <h2>Try it with your full salary</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/">calculator</a> works out your Labour Code wages from your CTC and shows gratuity
    alongside PF and ESI. In advanced mode you can set your years of service and whether you are on
    a fixed-term contract.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sources: Code on Social Security, 2020, s.53, in force from 21 November 2025; Industrial
    Relations Code, 2020, s.2(o) on fixed-term employment; Ministry of Labour and Employment FAQs of
    30 December 2025 (Q12, the maximum) and Additional FAQs of 16 March 2026 (Q10, Q14 and Q16).{' '}
    <a href="/how-it-works">Full method</a>.
  </p>
</DocLayout>

<script>
  import { mountGratuity } from '../scripts/gratuity';
  mountGratuity();
</script>
